Bug 2057710. In moz icon protocal code, strip the placeholder file name's leading period before sending it to other code that does not expect it. r=gfx-reviewers,maxe,lsalzman

nsIMozIconURI::fileExtension is a placeholder file name rather than a bare
extension: it keeps the leading period so callers can use it directly as a
stand-in for a file that may not exist. The Windows backend depends on this,
handing the value to SHGetFileInfo as a fake file name, and a lone "." is a
legitimate value meaning the name has no extension.

Two consumers passed that value straight to nsIMIMEService, which expects a bare
extension. On Windows, getPrimaryExtension() with "." stripped the period and
then called nsMIMEInfoBase::AppendExtension() with an empty string, tripping its
assertion. On Linux, getTypeFromExtension() could never match the dotless tables
it consults first, so extension lookups only succeeded by way of
nsGIOService::GetMimeTypeFromExtension() building "file." + ".html".

Convert at both boundaries, and document the convention along with the
conversion it requires on the attribute itself.

Differential Revision: https://phabricator.services.mozilla.com/D314600


